WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.500
necodoc: from the obligee's PDF to a renewal run.

2
00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:05.667
Start from the bond form exactly as the obligee publishes it.

3
00:00:05.667 --> 00:00:09.333
AI proposes a box for each blank it finds, with a name and a type.

4
00:00:09.333 --> 00:00:12.500
An editor confirms each box in the overlay editor, with a live preview.

5
00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:16.167
In a designed template, a section can show only when it applies.

6
00:00:16.167 --> 00:00:19.500
Tick "Add a co-principal" and the bond gets a second principal and a second signature block.

7
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:22.500
The person filling it in gets a guided form, one step at a time.

8
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:25.500
The same renderer makes the preview and the finished PDF.

9
00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:28.500
Your policy system can also post HTML and data to the API and get the PDF back.

10
00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:31.500
Measured median: 30 ms for a 3-page invoice with a header and footer.

11
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:35.167
Month-end renewals: one batch of 5,000 rows.

12
00:00:35.167 --> 00:00:39.500
More workers, more throughput: 8,786 documents a minute on 4 workers, 5,950 on one. No licence fee per worker.

13
00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:42.833
E-signature ready: each PDF comes with its signers and the position of every signature box.

14
00:00:42.833 --> 00:00:45.500
Your DocuSign, Dropbox Sign or Adobe Acrobat Sign account runs the signing.

15
00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:48.167
Every change and sign-in goes into an append-only audit log.

16
00:00:48.167 --> 00:00:50.333
The generation log keeps every PDF with its input, output and timing.

17
00:00:50.333 --> 00:00:52.500
necodoc runs in your cloud. PDFs stay in your S3-compatible bucket or Azure Blob container.

18
00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:56.500
necodoc. Document generation for insurers, in your own cloud. Self-hosted, open source under MIT. github.com/mikevalstar/necodoc
